# {{.ModulePath}}

A gopernicus host application, scaffolded by `gopernicus init`. It is an
sdk-only composition root: a logger, an HTTP router with the standard
middleware, a `GET /healthz` liveness probe, and graceful shutdown. It mounts no
pockets yet — that is your first edit.

## What was scaffolded

```
cmd/server/main.go              composition root — wiring; the only place that names concrete adapters
Makefile                        build/vet/test/run/migrate + the host guard subset
.env.example                    environment template (copy to .env)
workshop/migrations/{{.LedgerDir}}/         host-owned migration ledger (scaffold-and-own, never-renumber){{if .HasDB}}
workshop/migrations/main.go     host-owned pre-boot migration runner (apply + -status){{end}}
```
{{if .HasDB}}
This host wires the **{{.DB}}** datastore connector. `main.go` opens it from the
environment and probes it in `/healthz`; the migration runner applies the
host-owned ledger before boot.
{{else}}
This host wires **no datastore** (`--db=none`): the store you supply — a real
connector or an in-memory implementation — is your choice when you mount a
pocket.
{{end}}
## First run

1. Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and fill it in. The runtime mode is never
   written there: `APP_RUNTIME_MODE` (`development` | `production`) is read from
   the process environment only, and a `.env` that assigns it is refused at
   boot with `environment.ErrModeFromFile`.
2. `make run` exports `APP_RUNTIME_MODE=development`; a direct run prefixes it:
   `APP_RUNTIME_MODE=development go run ./cmd/server`.
{{- if eq .DB "pgx"}}
3. `.env.example` asks for verified TLS (`DB_SSLMODE=verify-full`). A plain
   docker Postgres has none: in `.env`, comment out `DB_SSLMODE=verify-full`
   and uncomment `DB_SSLMODE=disable` (the first assignment wins). Production
   refuses it.
{{- end}}

## Resolving the framework

The `github.com/gopernicus/gopernicus/*` modules resolve the normal Go way from
their tagged releases (`v0.1.0` and later) — `go mod tidy` fetches them; nothing
extra to configure.

Then `make run` (or `APP_RUNTIME_MODE=development go run ./cmd/server`) and hit
`http://localhost:{{.Port}}/healthz`.

### Local framework development

To develop against a local gopernicus checkout instead of the tags, point each
module at it with a replace directive (drop these before relying on tagged
resolution again):

```
go mod edit -replace github.com/gopernicus/gopernicus/sdk=/abs/path/to/gopernicus/sdk
{{- if .HasDB}}
go mod edit -replace {{.ConnectorPath}}=/abs/path/to/gopernicus/{{.ConnectorRel}}
{{- end}}
go mod tidy
```

## Wiring a pocket

Add the pocket module (and its store adapter, if it persists data) to `go.mod`
at its tagged version, then wire it in the "mount your pockets here" block in
`cmd/server/main.go`. Construct its services with the required inputs and
optional `With...` options documented by that pocket. Hosts may call public use
cases directly from handlers or workers; no init() registration or service
locator is involved.

For a pocket that supplies HTTP components, mount them on the host router:

```go
if err := components.Register(pockets.Mount{Router: router, Logger: log}); err != nil {
	return err
}
```

## Migrations

Migrations are host-owned and applied pre-boot — the framework never migrates at
startup.
{{if .HasDB}}
```
gopernicus db create <slug>              # scaffold NNNN_<slug>.sql into the ledger
make migrate                             # apply pending migrations
APP_RUNTIME_MODE=development go run ./workshop/migrations -status
                                         # applied vs pending (file-only when the
                                         # database is unreachable)
```
{{- if eq .DB "pgx"}}

`DB_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT` and `DB_LOCK_TIMEOUT` (`pgxdb.Config.StatementTimeout`
and `LockTimeout`) bound every statement and lock wait on the server's pooled
connections; `.env.example` ships them commented out. They are for the server
process only: the runner zeroes both after it parses the environment, so an
index build or a migration queued behind live traffic is never cut short. Set
each in one place: a DSN that also names `statement_timeout` or `lock_timeout`
beside the key is refused at `Open`. Behind PgBouncer in transaction mode
(which refuses unknown startup parameters) leave both unset and use
`ALTER ROLE <runtime role> SET statement_timeout = '30s'` instead.
{{- end}}
{{else}}
This host has no datastore, so no runner was scaffolded. Re-run `gopernicus init
--db=turso|pgx` for a host with a migration runner.
{{end}}
