## Production posture

Set `APP_RUNTIME_MODE=production` in the process environment — systemd
`Environment=`/`EnvironmentFile=`, `docker --env-file`, or platform secrets;
production never reads `.env`, so every variable it carried moves there too.
In production this host refuses to boot when:

- `APP_RUNTIME_MODE` is unset or not exactly `development` or `production`;
- `PUBLIC_BASE_URL` is unset, not `https`, or not a bare origin
  (`web.ServerConfig.ValidateProduction`);
{{- if eq .DB "pgx"}}
- a database target is not verified TLS — `sslmode=verify-full` (or
  `verify-ca` with `sslrootcert`); a unix socket counts as local — or query
  logging is on (`pgxdb.ErrTLSRequired`, `pgxdb.ErrQueryLoggingRefused`);
{{- end}}
{{- if eq .DB "turso"}}
- the Turso URL is not TLS (`libsql://`, `https://`, or `wss://` without
  `tls=0`; a `file:` database is local) (`turso.ErrTLSRequired`);
{{- end}}
- a pocket you mount meets one of its own development-only options: pass
  every pocket this same `mode`, and write any waiver as a literal
  acknowledgement beside the thing it waives.

TLS terminates at your edge (load balancer, ingress, platform router); the app
sends HSTS and the browser defaults through `web.SecurityHeaders()`, and
`TRUSTED_PROXY_COUNT` names the proxies in front of it so `web.TrustProxies`
resolves the real client IP.

Check the composition before every deploy. This scaffold pins no tool, so CI
runs the guard directly:

```
go run github.com/gopernicus/gopernicus/workshop/gopernicus@v0.6.0 guard --posture .
```

It exits 0 clean and 1 with findings, and prints one `ack` line per waiver you
wrote. Exit 2 is a broken invocation — a release older than v0.5.0 does not
know `--posture` — so CI treats it as a failure, never a pass.
{{- if eq .DB "pgx"}}

### Audit tables and the two database roles

The pgx stores with an audit table (authentication `security_events`,
authorization `iam_audit` and `iam_operations`, secrets `secrets_audit`) need
two database roles, created with SQL rather than a provider console (console
roles can carry `pg_write_all_data`):

- a **migrator** that owns the schema, the tables, their sequences and the
  trigger functions, and runs `make migrate`;
- a **runtime** role the server connects as. On the audit tables it gets
  `SELECT` and `INSERT`, plus `USAGE` on their sequences; `UPDATE`, `DELETE`,
  `TRUNCATE` and `TRIGGER` are revoked — except that `DELETE` stays granted on
  `iam_operations`, whose trigger decides which rows may go.

Each store enforces this when it is constructed. The contract, in one
sentence: **in production a store refuses to boot when its runtime role is
superuser, owns an audit table, can UPDATE/DELETE/TRUNCATE/TRIGGER it without
the verified triggers, or cannot insert into it.** The immutability trigger
files are migrations — delete them from your ledger before applying if you
decline them.

Before every deploy, list each mounted pgx store's `AuditTables()` in
`postureTables` (`workshop/migrations/main.go`), run the preflight as the
migrator against the runtime role, and archive its output with the release:

```
APP_RUNTIME_MODE=production go run ./workshop/migrations -posture-role <runtime role>
```

It prints one line per table —
`table=<t> protected_by=<privilege|trigger|waiver|none> owner=… insert=… sequence_usage=… trigger=<state>`
— and exits non-zero when any table is unprotected or cannot be appended to.
{{- end}}

## Retention

The pockets ship bounded disposal calls and start none of them: the host
schedules each one for the pockets it mounts, from one runner instance per
database. This scaffold mounts no pocket, so this is the recipe, not code.

| pocket | call it from the host's schedule | what it bounds |
|---|---|---|
| authentication | `Components.Maintenance.Run(ctx)` in process (hourly, jittered), or `Components.Maintenance.Pass(ctx)` from a jobs schedule | with `Repositories.Maintenance`: expired sessions, OAuth states, grants, contact changes, challenges and OAuth2 rows, terminal invitations, and once a day the `security_events` rail past 400 days, archived then purged |
| authorization | `Components.Mutations.ExpireOperations(ctx, limit)` | with `Repositories.OperationExpiry`: operation records past `WithOperationRetention` (180 days by default, at least 7); keep it above every producer's retry window |
| jobs | `Components.Queue.PurgeTerminalJobs(ctx, before, limit)`; `PurgeTerminal` for the fenced queue | completed and dead-lettered jobs; keep `before` behind the scheduler's longest retry window |
| events | `outbox.PurgePublished(ctx, before)` | published outbox rows — unbounded, so run it as its own step, after a one-off manual drain of the backlog |
| cms | `PurgeBefore(ctx, before, limit)` on the `messaging.InquiryEraser` wired as `Repositories.InquiryMaintenance` | contact inquiries (third-party names, emails, messages) |
{{- if eq .DB "pgx"}}
| pgxdb rate limiter | `DELETE FROM ratelimit_windows WHERE expires_at < now();` from a host cron | expired rate-limit windows |
{{- end}}

Once the authentication runner runs, say so with a literal in the composition
root —
`authentication.WithMaintenance(authentication.MaintenanceConfig{MaintenanceRunnerAcknowledged: true})`;
production logs one `maintenance_runner=unacknowledged` WARN while it is false,
and `guard --posture` (workshop/gopernicus v0.7.0 and later) prints one `ack`
line for it. A host enforcing retention
through its own SQL cron leaves it false and accepts the WARN.
{{- if eq .DB "pgx"}}

The rail's floor is an invariant. The authentication store's
`0024_security_events_retention.sql` gives `security_events` its only purge
path, `security_events_purge_before`, a `SECURITY DEFINER` function the
migrator owns; it refuses any cutoff younger than 365 days, and nothing lowers
that floor. The migration cannot know your runtime role, so grant it, and keep
the schema and the migrator clean for the boot probe:

```sql
GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION security_events_purge_before(pg_catalog.timestamptz, pg_catalog.int4) TO <runtime role>;
REVOKE CREATE ON SCHEMA public FROM PUBLIC;
```

Run `make migrate` as a `NOSUPERUSER` role that owns the tables: a superuser
owner makes the function inoperative (one WARN at boot), and without the
`GRANT EXECUTE` the server boots with one `maintenance_execute=missing` WARN
and the rail step reports `forbidden` every pass. With the store's
`AuditTables()` in `postureTables`, `-posture-role` also prints
`functions=[security_events_purge_before:execute=… unsafe=… inoperative=…] floor=365 days`;
archive that output with the release.

Apply the retention migrations in their own runner invocation —
authentication `0023_retention.sql` and `0024_security_events_retention.sql`,
authorization `0007_iam_operations_expiry.sql`, jobs
`0005_job_queue_terminal_purge.sql`, cms `0022_inquiries_created_at.sql`, for
the stores you mount. Their indexes are plain `CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS`
builds, which block writes to the table while they run; on a large host
pre-build each one first, outside the runner, under the same name, then apply
the file (the in-stream build is a no-op):

```sql
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY IF NOT EXISTS idx_sessions_expires_at ON sessions (expires_at);
-- … each index the file creates, then confirm none is left invalid:
SELECT c.relname FROM pg_index i JOIN pg_class c ON c.oid = i.indexrelid WHERE NOT i.indisvalid;
```

A failed concurrent build leaves an INVALID index that `IF NOT EXISTS` keeps —
`DROP INDEX` it first, then build again.
{{- end}}
